GRAMMAR += [
 {"id": "g13", "t": "Tense review: aspect and voice", "lvl": "B2–C1", "body": "Every English verb form combines three choices. <b>Time</b>: past, present or future. <b>Aspect</b>: simple (a complete fact or habit), continuous (in progress, temporary, unfinished) or perfect (connected to a later time). <b>Voice</b>: active or passive. Choose the aspect for the meaning you want, not because of a 'signal word'.", "ex": ["I've lived here for three years. (still true now)", "I lived there for three years. (finished)", "She's been working too hard lately. (recent, ongoing activity)", "The bridge was being repaired when we arrived. (passive, in progress)", "By the time you read this, I'll have left.", "The results have just been published."], "q": [["They are building a new school. (passive)", "A new school is being built."], ["I started learning English six years ago and I still learn it. (present perfect continuous)", "I have been learning English for six years."], ["When I arrived, the film started already. (correct the tense)", "When I arrived, the film had already started."]]},
 {"id": "g14", "t": "Adverbs and adjective order", "lvl": "B2–C1", "body": "Some adverbs have two forms with different meanings: <b>hard / hardly</b>, <b>late / lately</b>, <b>near / nearly</b>, <b>high / highly</b>, <b>free / freely</b>. Strong adverbs go with strong adjectives (<i>absolutely exhausted</i>, not <i>very exhausted</i>). Opinion adjectives come before fact adjectives, usually in the order: opinion – size – age – shape – color – origin – material – purpose.", "ex": ["He works hard, but he hardly ever takes a break.", "I haven't seen her lately; she arrived late again.", "It's highly unlikely. The plane flew high above the clouds.", "I was absolutely furious. / I was very angry.", "a lovely little old wooden house", "an expensive new Italian leather sofa"], "q": [["I was very exhausted. (correct it)", "I was absolutely exhausted."], ["a wooden old beautiful table (correct the order)", "a beautiful old wooden table"], ["I've been very busy late. (correct it)", "I've been very busy lately."]]},
 {"id": "g15", "t": "Verb patterns: infinitive or -ing", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Some verbs take <b>to + infinitive</b> (decide, refuse, manage, afford), some take <b>-ing</b> (avoid, enjoy, keep, mind, consider), and some take both with a <b>change of meaning</b>: <b>remember/forget/regret/stop/try/go on</b>. With <b>remember, forget, regret</b>: -ing looks back at the past, to-infinitive looks forward to a duty.", "ex": ["I stopped smoking. (I gave up) / I stopped to smoke. (in order to)", "Remember to lock the door. / I remember locking the door.", "Try turning it off and on again. (an experiment) / I tried to lift it. (difficult)", "We regret to inform you … / I regret saying that.", "She went on talking. / She went on to become a doctor.", "Prices keep improving? No: Things keep getting better."], "q": [["I'll never forget the day I met you. (use 'meeting')", "I'll never forget meeting you."], ["Don't forget: post the letter. (Don't forget …)", "Don't forget to post the letter."], ["He was talking and didn't stop. (He went on …)", "He went on talking."]]},
 {"id": "g16", "t": "Modals of speculation (present and past)", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Show how sure you are with modals. Present: <b>must / might / may / could / can't + infinitive</b> (or + be + -ing). Past: <b>must / might / may / could / can't / couldn't + have + past participle</b>. Use <b>should have / shouldn't have</b> to criticize a past action, and <b>needn't have</b> for something done that wasn't necessary.", "ex": ["She must be at home: the lights are on.", "He can't be serious!", "They might be waiting at the station.", "You must have been exhausted after the flight.", "She can't have seen us; she didn't wave.", "You shouldn't have told him. / I needn't have brought an umbrella."], "q": [["I'm sure he forgot. (must)", "He must have forgotten."], ["It's impossible that she said that. (can't)", "She can't have said that."], ["It was a mistake to buy it. (shouldn't)", "I shouldn't have bought it."]]},
 {"id": "g17", "t": "Relative clauses", "lvl": "B2–C1", "body": "<b>Defining</b> clauses identify which person or thing (no commas; <i>that</i> is possible; the pronoun can be left out when it is the object). <b>Non-defining</b> clauses add extra information (commas; never <i>that</i>; never left out). <b>Which</b> can refer to a whole clause. Formal English puts prepositions before <b>whom/which</b>.", "ex": ["The man (who/that) I met was a doctor.", "My brother, who lives in Canada, is a doctor.", "He failed the exam, which surprised everyone.", "The person to whom I spoke was very helpful.", "That's the town where I grew up.", "The reason why I'm calling is …"], "q": [["My sister lives in Riga. She is a teacher. (non-defining)", "My sister, who lives in Riga, is a teacher."], ["He didn't call me. That annoyed me. (which)", "He didn't call me, which annoyed me."], ["The woman I spoke to was helpful. (formal, with whom)", "The woman to whom I spoke was helpful."]]},
 {"id": "g18", "t": "Discourse markers and attitude adverbs", "lvl": "C1", "body": "Discourse markers show how ideas connect and how you feel about them. <b>Attitude:</b> honestly, frankly, admittedly, surprisingly, unfortunately, obviously. <b>Contrast or limit:</b> mind you, still, all the same, even so. <b>Giving a reason:</b> after all. <b>Changing topic:</b> anyway, by the way, as for … <b>Summing up:</b> basically, in short.", "ex": ["Frankly, I think it's a waste of money.", "It's expensive. Mind you, it is very good quality.", "Don't be too hard on him. After all, he's only six.", "Anyway, that's enough about me.", "Admittedly, I didn't try very hard.", "It rained all week. Still, we had a good time."], "q": [["Add a marker that limits the point: 'The hotel was noisy. ___, the staff were lovely.'", "Mind you / Still / Even so, the staff were lovely."], ["Add a marker giving a reason: 'Let him go. ___, it's his birthday.'", "After all, it's his birthday."]]},
]

